course: everyday
lesson: 247
title: Running games and the playground
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Running games are safest when everybody follows the same rules: take turns, wait until the space is clear, and play fair even when you are out.
explain: Some dangers are hidden: a lollipop stick in the mouth or a pencil in the hand can stab you if you fall, and a rope or hoodie cord can catch on equipment and strangle.
explain: Monkey bars are where most playground injuries happen, so you use both hands and go one way, and a metal slide in the sun can burn, so you touch it first.
explain: Outside, you come home when the street lights come on or at the agreed time, because drivers cannot see you in the dark and your parents will worry.
explain: A girl in Phoenix touches the slide with her hand on a hot July afternoon, feels it is burning hot, and chooses the swings instead.
fact: When someone swings a golf club, you stand well back and to the side, because a club or ball can cause a serious head injury.
fact: You come home when the street lights come on or at the agreed time.
fact: In the dark drivers cannot see you well, and parents worry.
fact: Never run with a lollipop or anything in your mouth, or with sharp things in your hand.
fact: In a relay you pass the baton into the next runner's hand; in a three-legged race you start slowly and move together.
fact: In games like red light green light or Simon says you follow the caller and accept being out.
fact: If a game gets too rough, you say stop, and tell an adult if it does not stop.
fact: On a trampoline or busy playground you take turns and wait until the space is clear.
fact: On monkey bars you use both hands, everyone goes the same way, and nobody hangs upside down without a soft surface below.
fact: Falls from monkey bars are the commonest playground injury.
fact: A metal slide, or a dark plastic one, in the summer sun can burn skin, so you touch it with your hand first.
fact: Ropes, scarves and hoodie cords can catch on equipment and strangle, so they never go near climbing frames.
fact: If someone keeps the swing for ages, you ask politely for a turn or ask an adult to help.
exercise: An adult is playing golf. Where do you stand? | Well back and to the side | Right behind the golfer | Just in front of the ball | Close beside the club
exercise: Why is it dangerous to stand near someone swinging a golf club? | The club or ball can cause a serious head injury | The golfer will lose the game because you put him off his swing | The grass might get damaged | The ball will go much too far and get lost in the trees
exercise: You are playing in the street and the street lights come on. What do you do? | Go home | Play until you are tired | Hide so nobody finds you | Play in the road where it is lit
exercise: Why should you not run with a lollipop in your mouth? | A fall could push the stick into your throat | It makes you run much slower than everyone else | The lollipop melts faster | It is bad for your teeth to have sugar in your mouth while running
exercise: In a relay race, how do you pass the baton? | Into the next runner's hand | Throw it to them from far away | Drop it on the ground for them | Roll it along the track to them
exercise: How do you start a three-legged race well? | Slowly, moving together | As fast as you can | Each leg at its own speed | By pulling your partner
exercise: In Simon says, you move when Simon did not say "Simon says". What do you do? | Accept that you are out | Argue that you did not move | Keep playing and hope nobody saw | Leave the game crossly
exercise: A game of tag gets too rough and someone is pushing hard. What do you do? | Say stop and tell an adult if it does not stop | Push back harder so they know how it feels | Keep playing and say nothing at all to anyone | Start a fight
exercise: At a trampoline park, several children want the same trampoline. What is safe? | Take turns and wait until it is clear | Jump on all together so nobody has to wait | Jump on while someone else is still landing | Push someone off
exercise: How do you go across the monkey bars? | Using both hands, one way | With one hand to look cool | Hanging upside down over concrete | Towards someone coming the other way
exercise: What is the commonest playground injury? | Falls from monkey bars | Splinters from benches | Stings from bees | Scrapes from sandpits
exercise: On a sunny summer day you want to go down the metal slide. What first? | Touch it with your hand | Sit down quickly | Pour sand on it | Go down head first
exercise: You are wearing a hoodie with long cords. What should you do before climbing? | Take the cords out or tuck them away | Tie them to the bar so they help you balance | Wrap them round your neck twice so they stay out of the way | Nothing, they are harmless
exercise: Your friend wants to tie a skipping rope to the top of the climbing frame. What do you say? | No, ropes on equipment can strangle | Yes, it makes a really good swing for everyone | Yes, but tie it a lot tighter | Yes, as long as you get to go on it first
exercise: A girl has been on the only swing for a very long time. What do you do? | Ask politely for a turn or ask an adult | Push her off the swing | Grab the chains hard to make her stop | Stand right in front of the swing until she gets off
test: Your mum said to be home at six, and the game is exciting at five to six. What should you do? | Go home at the agreed time | Stay ten more minutes | Stay until it gets dark | Ask a friend to phone mum
test: A boy in Melbourne runs to the bus holding a sharpened pencil. What could happen if he trips? | The pencil could stab his eye or body | The pencil would just break without hurting anyone | He would run faster | Nothing, because pencils are never really sharp enough to hurt
test: In the school relay, Ava throws the baton to her teammate and it falls. What went wrong? | The baton should be passed into the hand | She should have thrown it higher and harder | Batons should always be rolled along the ground | Only the teacher may pass it
test: In red light green light, Jack moves on red but says he did not. Why does this spoil the game? | The game only works when people play fair | Jack will run too fast for the others to catch up | The caller will get tired | Red light green light has no real rules, so nothing is spoilt
test: A girl in Colorado sees that the climbing frame has soft bark under it but the bars over the path have concrete. Where should she not hang upside down? | Over the concrete | Over the soft bark | Anywhere with mats | Over sand
test: Why can a scarf be dangerous on a slide or climbing frame? | It can catch and strangle you | It makes you slide too slowly | It gets dirty | It keeps you too warm
test: Two children run onto the trampoline while another is still jumping. What is likely? | They collide and get hurt | They all bounce higher safely | The trampoline gets stronger | Nothing happens to anyone at all
test: A wrestling game turns into real hitting and the other child does not stop when you say so. What next? | Tell an adult | Hit back harder | Keep quiet so you are not called a tattletale | Go home and never tell anyone
test: On a hot August day in Florida, a boy sits straight down on a black plastic slide in full sun. What might happen? | He could burn his legs | The slide would feel cold | He would slide too slowly | Nothing at all
test: street lights just came on but we're mid-game | Go home now, like you agreed | Finish the game first | Stay out till it's really dark | Hide so mum can't find you
test: tag's getting really rough, kids shoving | Say stop, and tell an adult if it doesn't stop | Shove back even harder so they stop | Keep playing and hope it stops by itself | Run away and say nothing
test: wanna hang upside down on the monkey bars, it's just concrete under | No, not without a soft surface below | Yes, if you hold on tight | Yes, concrete is fine for a short time | Only if a friend catches you
test: running to the bus stop w a lolly in my mouth lol | Take it out first, never run with it in your mouth | Run faster so it's over sooner | Bite it in half and keep running, then it's short | Hold it tight with your teeth only, no hands
explain: A boy in Texas ran backwards in a game of tag and hit a metal pole; he got a concussion (a brain injury from a hard knock to the head) and was sick and dizzy for days.
explain: A girl in Canada played chase near the school gate; she ran out after a friend between parked cars and was hit by a car, and broke her leg.
fact: Head injuries from running into poles, walls and other children are common in playgrounds; a child with a head bump who is sleepy, sick or confused needs a doctor.
fact: Children who run between parked cars are hard for drivers to see until it is too late to stop.
test: chase near the gate, it's more fun there | Play away from the gate and the road | Fine if you look first | Fine, cars go slow by schools | Only if a friend keeps watch
test: mate bumped heads with me, now he's sleepy and feels sick | Tell an adult now, he needs checking | Let him have a nap n sleep it off | Get him to run it off round the field | Give him a fizzy drink n a biscuit
test: Why are so many crashes in tag about running backwards? | You can't see what's behind you | Running backwards is always much slower | Poles move about in the playground | Tag is only really safe when everyone runs backwards
test: did u hear a kid ran out between parked cars after a ball and a van didn't see him? real lesson? | Don't run out between parked cars; drivers can't see you | Run fast so the van can't hit you | Only vans are dangerous, not cars | Parked cars protect you from traffic
test: apparently a boy fell off the monkey bars and broke his arm in 5 places, my mate saw the bone. what's the true lesson? | Use both hands and go the same way; falls are common | Monkey bars should be banned everywhere | Hang upside down for a better grip | Only small kids fall off monkey bars
test: my mate says a kid ran with a lolly in his mouth and the stick went through his throat. probably exaggerated, but the lesson? | Never run with anything in your mouth | Lollies are fine if they're the small ones | Run slowly with lollies and it's ok | Only sharp sweets are dangerous to run with, not lollies
exam: Identify the most common cause of playground injury. | Falls from monkey bars | Running on grass | Sitting on benches | Drinking from fountains
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate response when a game becomes too rough? | To say stop and to tell an adult if it continues | To become rougher in return | To continue so as not to spoil the game for the others | To leave without saying anything to anyone whatever
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice concerning a metal slide in summer sun. | It is touched with the hand first, as it may burn | It is used at once, since metal stays cool in the sun | It is cooled with a cold drink | It is sat upon without testing
exam: Ascertain the signs after a head bump that require medical attention. | Sleepiness, sickness or confusion | A good appetite and a cheerful smile | Wishing to play again at once with friends | A small pink mark that fades within the hour
